  "summary": "The fix for RUSTSEC-2026-0185 in 0.11.15 added a cap on the number of chunks buffered by the",
  "details": "The fix for RUSTSEC-2026-0185 in 0.11.15 added a cap on the number of chunks buffered by the\n`Assembler` that reassembles out-of-order stream data. However, the cap was only checked inside\nthe branch that triggers defragmentation, which only runs when the estimated over-allocation\nexceeds a threshold proportional to the amount of buffered data. Frames handled entirely by the\nunordered-read deduplication loop returned before the check ran at all.\n\nA peer sending one small gapped STREAM frame per packet (for example, 6-byte frames separated by\n1-byte gaps) keeps the per-chunk overhead below the defragmentation threshold, so the cap is never\nevaluated and the chunk count grows until stream-level flow control stops the peer. With the default\n`TransportConfig` (100 concurrent unidirectional streams and a 1.25 MB stream receive window), a\nremote, unauthenticated peer can pin roughly 3 GB of receiver memory using about 125 MB of accounted\nstream data. The memory is not reclaimed until the affected streams or the connection are closed.\n\nThe flaw was corrected in 0.11.17 by checking the chunk count after every insert (including in the\nunordered deduplication loop) and compacting the buffer once it passes a watermark above the cap.\nA stream is rejected once compaction can no longer bring the chunk count back under the cap.\n\nVersions before 0.11.15 are affected by the original, broader issue described in RUSTSEC-2026-0185.\n\nThanks to Otto Hermann ([@ojhermann](https://github.com/ojhermann)) for reporting and fixing\nthis issue.",
  "severity": [
    {
      "type": "CVSS_V3",
      "score": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H"
